<h4>Microbiologist</h4>
<p>Dr. Dabral</p>
<h4>Comments</h4>
<p>01/27/2026<br>
Mixed bacterial growth. 4+ growth. Identification in progress.<br>
1- 3+ growth.<br>
2- 4+ growth.<br>
3- 2+ growth.<br>
JW/GSR</p>
<p>01/28/2026<br>
1,2,3- Antimicrobial susceptibility testing is in progress for this organism.<br>
4,10- 3+ growth.<br>
JW/GSR</p>
<p>01/29/2026<br>
2- The only veterinary breakpoints for Enterococcus species are for ampicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, and<br>
chloramphenicol in dogs in the urine and skin and soft tissue and ampicillin and amoxicillin-clavulanate<br>
in cats in the urine. For dogs, the Staphylococcus breakpoints are used for enrofloxacin, marbofloxacin,<br>
and pradofloxacin. All other drugs listed here are interpreted with human breakpoints. Isolates that are<br>
susceptible to penicillin and benzylpenicillin will be susceptible to amoxicillin, amoxicillin with<br>
clavulanate, and other aminopenicillin drugs. In accordance with published guidelines of CLSI (Clinical<br>
Laboratory Standards Institute), susceptibility testing for enterococci against aminoglycosides,<br>
cephalosporins, sulfonamides, and clindamycin have not been reported because of lack of clinical<br>
correlation.</p>
<p>3- The isolate belongs to carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE) and resistant to penicillins,<br>
cephalosporins, monobactams, and carbapenems. In general, CRE is of particular concern and<br>
epidemiological relevance because carbapenemase genes are frequently located on mobile genetic<br>
elements and therefore can be transferred horizontally between bacteria. Carbapenem resistance is<br>
also frequently associated with co-location of resistance genes for other antimicrobial classes, leaving<br>
very few alternatives for treating CRE-induced infection.</p>
<p>10- Antimicrobial susceptibility testing is in progress for this organism.</p>
<p>1,3- Please note, there are limited interpretive criteria (breakpoints) for Enterobacterales (E. coli and Entero. hormaechei) for cats.<br>
There are urine breakpoints for cats for ampicillin (predicts amoxicillin), amoxicillin-clavulanate, and<br>
cefovecin. These three breakpoints are dependent on the ability of the cat to concentrate its urine.<br>
Other breakpoints are based on systemic sites. For systemic sites in cats, there are breakpoints for<br>
ampicillin/amoxicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, cefovecin, enrofloxacin, marbofloxacin, orbifloxacin, and<br>
pradofloxacin. There are no other feline-specific breakpoints available. All other drugs are interpreted<br>
with either canine or human breakpoints. There are no canine or feline breakpoints for cefotaxime,<br>
erythromycin, nitrofurantoin, rifampin, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. These drugs were<br>
interpreted with human breakpoints. Please note, there are no canine interpretive criteria (breakpoints)<br>
for Enterobacterales (E. coli) for tetracycline but there are breakpoints for these drugs for dogs for<br>
other organisms which were used for this interpretation. Additionally, Enterobacterales are considered<br>
resistant to ampicillin and amoxicillin-clavulanate at sites other than the lower urinary tract. Isolates<br>
from the lower urinary tract are only considered susceptible if the patient is able to concentrate their<br>
urine and the minimum inhibitory concentration is in the susceptible range. This isolate would be<br>
considered resistant to ampicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate and cephalexin if encountered in the kidney.<br>
JW/GSR</p></div>        <strong>Clinic Notes / Specifics:</strong><br>
