            SA-US-Abdomen
                                <br><strong>Images:</strong><br>
                        
        <strong>Outcome:</strong><br>
        <div class="parsedown"><p>Lab Name: Imaging<br>
Lab Category: SA Ultrasound<br>
Accession Number: USSA26-014-007<br>
Order Status: Finalized<br>
Order Last Updated: 01/14/2026 4:39 PM</p>
<h4>Faculty*</h4>
<p>Gould</p>
<h4>House Officer*</h4>
<p>Jenkins</p>
<h4>Student Name</h4>
<p>Melody Grant</p>
<h4>Requesting Service*</h4>
<p>SA IM1</p>
<h4>Date of Procedure*</h4>
<p>2026-01-14T13:10</p>
<h4>Patient Weight (KG)*</h4>
<p>5.52</p>
<h4>Region of Interest Previously Imaged at TAMU?*</h4>
<p>( ) No</p>
<p>(X) Yes</p>
<h4>Did Referral Films / Images Arrive with Patient?*</h4>
<p>(X) No</p>
<p>( ) Yes</p>
<h4>Anesthetized*</h4>
<p>(X) No</p>
<p>( ) Yes</p>
<h4>Sedated*</h4>
<p>( ) No</p>
<p>(X) Yes</p>
<h4>Ward/Cage*</h4>
<p>1015-3 - 1015 ER Feline Ward</p>
<h4>Region*</h4>
<p>Abdomen</p>
<h4>What is the presenting complaint?*</h4>
<p>Regurgitation that is refractory to anti-emetics; weight loss, small bowel diarrhea;<br>
previously thickened intestines and enlarged lymph nodes on recent ultrasound here; stomach was also full on that ultrasound with fluid</p>
<h4>Are there any related physical examination, lameness and/or lab abnormalities?*</h4>
<p>mild anemia, inflammatory CBC/Chem, mild azotemia (recent treatment of lily ingestion)<br>
non-painful on abdominal palpation</p>
<h4>What is your working differential list?*</h4>
<p>IBD, lymphoma, histoplasmosis, esophageal motility disorder, megaesophagus</p>
<h4>What are your clinical questions?*</h4>
<p>Are the intestines or lymph nodes changed from the last ultrasound? We got approval for cystocentesis for urinalysis and aspiration of mesenteric lymph nodes if they warrant aspiration.</p>
<h4>What treatments are currently being administered?</h4>
<p>metaclopramide, maropitant, ondansetron, esomeprazole</p>
<h4>Is the patient suspected to have a zoonotic diseases?*</h4>
<p>Not at this time but GI zoonosis hasn't been fully ruled out</p>
<h4>Are there any precautions related to the patient?*</h4>
<p>Careful with positioning because last time he regurgitated multiple times when trying to place him on his back; we are trying a different sedation protocol this time</p>
<h4>Are there any special considerations related to scheduling?</h4>
<p>Today is requested but no other constraints</p>
<h4>Are there any special instructions/views?</h4>
<p>Cystocentesis, aspirate of mesenteric lymph nodes if it is warranted based on imaging</p>
<p>Fields with an asterisk are required.</p>
<h4>Senior Clinician</h4>
<p>Sydney Gibson</p>
<h4>Interpreted By</h4>
<p>Jessica Repep</p>
<h3>Imaging Interpretation</h3>
<h4>Technical Factors</h4>
<p>Complete abdominal ultrasound (99 still images and 4 cine loops) performed on January 14, 2026.<br>
Previous study dated January 1, 2026 is available for comparison.</p>
<h4>Imaging Findings</h4>
<ul>
<li>HEPATOBILIARY: Moderate volume echogenic debris within the gallbladder lumen.</li>
<li>SPLEEN: No abnormalities.</li>
<li>UROGENITAL: No abnormalities.</li>
<li>ADRENAL GLANDS: No abnormalities.<br>
Left: 3.79 mm<br>
Right: 3.18 mm   </li>
<li>GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT: Similar moderate diffuse thickening of the small intestinal muscularis layer.</li>
<li>PANCREAS: No abnormalities.</li>
<li>LYMPH NODES: Similar mild-moderate enlargement and moderate hypoechogenicity jejunal lymph nodes (up to 8.02 mm).  Mild hypoechogenicity of the left colic lymph node (3.27 mm).  Mild enlargement of the right medial iliac lymph node (6.13 mm).</li>
<li>VASCULATURE: No abnormalities.</li>
<li>OTHER: None.</li>
<li>COMMENTS: Under ultrasound guidance, a 1.5-inch-long, 22-gauge needle was inserted percutaneously into the lumen of the urinary bladder.  Approximately 6 mL of clear, colorless fluid was withdrawn.  Subsequently, the patient was resedated and the right abdomen was aseptically prepared with chlorhexidine and isopropyl alcohol.  Under ultrasound guidance, a 1.5-inch-long, 22-gauge needles were inserted percutaneously into the largest identified jejunal lymph node (three samples), and direct-prep slides were prepared. No complications were identified at either site.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Imaging Conclusions</h4>
<ol>
<li>Small intestinal muscularis thickening and multifocal intra-abdominal lymphadenopathy, again consistent with chronic enteropathy such as inflammatory bowel disease or small cell GI lymphoma and secondary lymphadenopathy (reactive or multicentric neoplasia).</li>
<li>Urocystocentesis and jejunal lymph node fine-needle aspiration without evidence of immediate complication.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Comments</h4>
<p>JRepep / SGibson<br>
Interpreted: January 14, 2026<br>
Case # 534870</p>
