            CP-Cytology-Cytologic Examination
                                <br><strong>Images:</strong><br>
                        
        <strong>Outcome:</strong><br>
        <div class="parsedown"><p>Result Notes:<br>
Lab Name: Clinical Pathology<br>
Lab Category: Cytology<br>
Accession Number: 26010668<br>
Order Status: Finalized<br>
Order Last Updated: 01/15/2026 9:24 AM</p>
<h2>Cytology Request</h2>
<h4>Priority</h4>
<p>Routine</p>
<p>[ ] Preliminary results needed by end of day</p>
<h4>Zoonotic Concern Type</h4>
<p>None</p>
<h4>Clinical Service *</h4>
<p>SA IM1</p>
<h1>|</h1>
<h4>Sample Collection Date &amp; Time *</h4>
<p>2026-01-14T15:38</p>
<h4>Specimen *</h4>
<p>Other</p>
<h4>Sample (Source) *</h4>
<p>Aspirate</p>
<h4>Specific location of sample *</h4>
<p>Jejunal Lymph node</p>
<h4>Additional Information</h4>
<h4>Pertinent History *</h4>
<p>Regurgitation that is refractory to anti-emetics; weight loss, small bowel diarrhea;<br>
previously thickened intestines and enlarged lymph nodes on recent ultrasound here; stomach was also full on that ultrasound with fluid</p>
<h3>Sample Information</h3>
<p>[ ] After-Hours submission (5pm or later)</p>
<h4>Sample Received</h4>
<p>Wednesday, 14 January 2026 15:46:00</p>
<h3>Technologist Area</h3>
<h4>Interpretations</h4>
<h4>Student Technician</h4>
<h4>Student Technician</h4>
<h4>Technologist</h4>
<h4>Technologist</h4>
<h4>Technologist Comments</h4>
<h3>Pathology Area</h3>
<h4>Resident</h4>
<h4>Pathologist</h4>
<p>Mary Nabity</p>
<h4>Pathologist Comments</h4>
<p>Description: Four smears made from aspirates of the jejunal lymph node are examined. The smears are moderately to highly cellular and moderately to markedly hemodiluted in a light purple proteinaceous background containing frequent lymphoglandular bodies, lysed cells, and platelet clumps. The nucleated cells are predominantly lymphocytes, with occasional macrophages and plasma cells. Neutrophils and eosinophils appear to be present in numbers consistent with the degree of hemodilution. The lymphocytes overall appear to be a heterogeneous population. The majority are small, typically with a mild to moderate left shift to intermediate and large lymphocytes. However, in some areas, intermediate and large lymphocytes approach 50% of the lymphoid population. Occasional mitotic figures are seen. Macrophages are often lightly vacuolated, and they rarely display erythrophagocytosis. No infectious agents or overt neoplastic cells are seen. </p>
<p>Interpretation: Moderate lymphoid hyperplasia</p>
<p>Comment: The left shift to immature lymphocytes without a concurrent increase in plasma cells in an enlarged lymph node is consistent with hyperplasia. There is no strong evidence for a neoplastic process given the overall heterogeneity of the lymphocytes; however, if clinically warranted, biopsy with histologic evaluation, PCR, and/or flow cytometry could be performed to further characterize the lymphoid population.  </p></div>        <strong>Clinic Notes / Specifics:</strong><br>
        <div class="parsedown"><p>81<br>
CP-Cytology-Cytologic Examination<br>
Lab</p>
<p>Result Date: Jan 14, 2026 11:12:22 PM</p></div><br>
