            <div class="" style="">
                <div style="margin:2px;overflow-y:auto;width:100%;">
            <div><b>Requested By:</b> Alyssa Jenkins DVM</div>
            <div><b>Supplier:</b> VMTH SA Radiology</div>
            <div><b>Reference:</b> US4276-DR20292</div>
            SA-RAD-Esophagram
                                <br><strong>Images:</strong><br>
                        
        <strong>Outcome:</strong><br>
        <div class="parsedown"><p>Lab Name: Imaging<br>
Lab Category: SA Radiography<br>
Accession Number: DXSA26-014-023<br>
Order Status: Finalized<br>
Order Last Updated: 01/15/2026 4:59 PM</p>
<h4>Faculty*</h4>
<p>Gould</p>
<h4>House Officer*</h4>
<p>Jenkins</p>
<h4>Student Name</h4>
<p>Melody</p>
<h4>Requesting Service*</h4>
<p>SA IM1</p>
<h4>Date of Procedure*</h4>
<p>2026-01-15T17:37</p>
<h4>Patient Weight (KG)*</h4>
<p>5.5</p>
<h4>Region of Interest Previously Imaged at TAMU?*</h4>
<p>( ) No</p>
<p>(X) Yes</p>
<h4>Did Referral Films / Images Arrive with Patient?*</h4>
<p>(X) No</p>
<p>( ) Yes</p>
<h4>Anesthetized*</h4>
<p>(X) No</p>
<p>( ) Yes</p>
<h4>Sedated*</h4>
<p>(X) No</p>
<p>( ) Yes</p>
<h4>Ward/Cage*</h4>
<p>Unknown</p>
<h4>Region*</h4>
<p>Thorax</p>
<h4>What is the presenting complaint?*</h4>
<p>Regurgitation</p>
<h4>Are there any related physical examination, lameness and/or lab abnormalities?*</h4>
<p>Regurgitation after eating; seems interested, but not keeping food down</p>
<h4>What is your working differential list?*</h4>
<p>Esophagitis vs. dysmotility vs. stricture vs. other</p>
<h4>What are your clinical questions?*</h4>
<p>Evidence of structural or functional esophageal issue?</p>
<h4>What treatments are currently being Administered?</h4>
<p>Metoclopramide, cisapride, esomeprazole, ondansetron, maropitant, fenbendazole</p>
<h4>Is the patient suspected to have a zoonotic diseases?*</h4>
<p>No</p>
<h4>Are there any precautions related to the patient?*</h4>
<p>No</p>
<h4>Are there any special considerations related to scheduling?</h4>
<p>No</p>
<h4>Are there any special instructions/views?*</h4>
<p>No; swallow study</p>
<p>Fields with an asterisk are required.</p>
<h4>Radiologist</h4>
<p>Zachary Bassett</p>
<h4>Interpreted By</h4>
<p>Elizabeth Munnerlyn</p>
<h3>Imaging Interpretation</h3>
<h4>Technical Factors</h4>
<p>A videofluoroscopic esophagram was performed on 01/15/2026 with a single (soft food) consistency.  The patient was free-fed in a plexiglass box without additional restraint.  6 cineloops are available for interpretation.  There are no previous fluoroscopic studies for comparison.</p>
<h4>Imaging Findings</h4>
<p>Survey: No abnormalities.</p>
<p>Soft food:  Ingesta is reliably delivered as boluses through the upper esophageal sphincter, with appropriate timing of pharyngeal contraction and upper esophageal sphincter relaxation; pharyngeal contraction is subjectively mildly decreased, though boluses are small and food consistency is very soft (near-liquid).  Boluses are conveyed by primary peristalsis to approximately the mid-cervical level.  </p>
<p>Further aborad progress occurs very gradually, without obvious esophageal muscular contraction.  Thoracic limbs summate with the thoracic inlet and cranial thorax, moderately impeding visualization of the esophagus at this location.  No discrete narrowing is identified, though the dispersed ingesta only mildly distends the esophagus in this region.</p>
<p>Bolus material reaching the level of the diaphragm appears to advance gradually but unimpeded through a lower esophageal sphincter approximately equal in diameter to the caudal esophagus (i.e., open).</p>
<p>In the final cineloop, the patient has regurgitated twice in the interim since the immediately previous cineloop.  Regurgitated material in the oropharynx is swallowed again, combining with a large volume of frothy contrast-impregnated ingesta in the cervical esophagus.  Starting at frame 72 of 363, the patient extends the head and neck, and the cranial aspect of the bolus moves orad through the upper esophageal sphincter that remains partially closed. A subsequent swallow that does not propel the material aborad is immediately followed by complete opening of the upper esophageal sphincter and further aborad movement of cervical esophageal content, which is then returned to the cranial cervical esophagus by a swallow.  In frame 191 the lower esophageal sphincter again opens and closes in conjunction with extension of the head and neck and starting at frame 200 this cycle repeats once more, associated with orad movement and disgorgement of a large volume of frothy fluid.  A small amount of barium contrast medium is seen on the ventral margin of the cranial cervical trachea in this cineloop.</p>
<h4>Imaging Conclusions</h4>
<ol>
<li>Severe and apparently diffuse esophageal dysmotility, with possible concurrent pharyngeal weakness. No discrete narrowing (stenosis, stricture) is identified, though the cranial thoracic esophagus (thoracic inlet to approximately the 4th rib) cannot be fully evaluated.</li>
<li>Mild aspiration of barium contrast medium, not expected to cause clinical signs.</li>
</ol>
<p>BBoudreau\ZB<br>
Interpreted 01/15/2026<br>
534870</p>
